Reason:
The thread leak occurs because `gfs2_destroy_threads()` is called only in the error block after `gfs2_make_fs_rw()` fails. When `gfs2_freeze_lock_shared()` fails (before `gfs2_make_fs_rw`), the code jumps directly to `fail_per_node`, which does not call `gfs2_destroy_threads()`, leaving kernel threads (logd, quotad) allocated. The quota bitmap leak occurs because after `gfs2_quota_init()` succeeds in `gfs2_make_fs_rw()`, if the filesystem is later withdrawn (e.g., due to another failure), the allocated quota bitmap buffer is never freed because `gfs2_quota_cleanup()` is missing from the error path.

The code statements that fix the bug are:
<text>
// In gfs2_fill_super:
fail_per_node:
    init_per_node(sdp, UNDO);
+   gfs2_destroy_threads(sdp);   // added here to cover all error paths
</text>
<text>
// In gfs2_make_fs_rw:
// After a failure that occurs after gfs2_quota_init() succeeds:
+   gfs2_quota_cleanup(sdp);
</text>
Reason:
Moving `gfs2_destroy_threads()` to the `fail_per_node` label ensures that kernel threads are destroyed regardless of where the error occurs after `init_threads()` succeeds. This handles the case where `gfs2_freeze_lock_shared()` fails and also replaces the previous placement inside the `gfs2_make_fs_rw` error block, making the cleanup uniform. Adding `gfs2_quota_cleanup()` in the error path of `gfs2_make_fs_rw()` properly frees the quota bitmap buffer when the filesystem fails to become read-write after quota initialization, preventing the memory leak.

